Subject: [PATCH 10708/11362] Write up the induce truncation finding: the tier
 is truncation-bound, not incompetent

Two live adapter-free runs show the generator reasoning at length and never
emitting the engine field: ls20 and wa30 returned reasoning_only 24 of 24 with
chars_final 0, r11l 41 of 42 with chars_final 3457. both_channels_empty is 0
throughout, so the server always answered.

The mechanism is already named in our own code. Three r11l induction attempts
failed with TRUNCATED BY SHARED CONTEXT POOL, generating 18431, 4066 and 2996 of a
26800-token budget, and _limit_diagnostic exists precisely to separate that from an
ordinary budget limit. Its docstring states the fix: raise -c or
CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_N_CTX, and raising max_tokens would make it worse.
last_prompt_truncated is false, so the prompt fits and the generation does not.

The defect worth fixing first is that the diagnostic never reaches the record
intact. The message is 216 characters; every stored copy is 179.
arc_executable_world_model.py:8314 stores
f"split induce: engine failed: {str(eng)[:150]}" -- a 29-character prefix plus a
150-character clip, and 29 + 150 = 179 matches all three observed strings exactly.
The tail that is cut carries the n_ctx pool size and the sentence naming the fix.
So the artifact records that truncation happened while dropping the numbers needed
to size it. The same file's line 5535 docstring notes two earlier sessions
re-derived an unread error body; the evidence is in the message here, and it is the
storage that clips it.

This also answers the tool-use question, which was the reason for looking. Tool use
was OFF in both runs, verified from /proc/<pid>/environ and consistent with
tool_loop_reinduction fired=0. Running the A/B now would not measure tools: tool
definitions lengthen the prompt, and prompt length is the exhausted quantity, so a
tool arm would likely score worse for a reason unrelated to tools and that would
enter the record as evidence against them.

Limits are stated in the note rather than implied. Prompt token counts are recorded
nowhere in the artifact, so the pool arithmetic is unmeasured, and nothing here
establishes that raising -c improves the solve rate. Two runs, three games, one
model, one server configuration.

No code changed and the running cd82 game was not touched.

